<h2>The Busy Timeout Pragma: Eliminating Database Locked Errors</h2>
<p>By default, when a SQLite connection attempts to begin a write transaction while another connection holds a write lock, SQLite immediately aborts the query and throws a "database is locked" error. For inexperienced developers, this default behavior creates the illusion that SQLite cannot handle concurrent requests.</p>
<p>The solution is setting the busy_timeout pragma: PRAGMA busy_timeout = 5000. This configuration instructs SQLite that if a lock cannot be obtained immediately, the engine must sleep and retry using an exponential backoff algorithm for up to 5,000 milliseconds before throwing an exception.</p>
<p>Because SQLite write transactions complete in microseconds, concurrent connections rarely wait more than two or three milliseconds before obtaining the lock. Under this configuration, transient write contention is smoothed out completely, and "database is locked" errors virtually disappear from production application logs.</p>
<h2>Synchronous Pragmas: Balancing Durability and Throughput</h2>
<p>By default, relational databases execute fsync() system calls during transaction commits to ensure that modified pages are physically flushed from drive caches onto non-volatile storage platters. Under PRAGMA synchronous = FULL, every transaction commit stalls until the physical storage acknowledges the write, which limits write throughput to a few hundred transactions per second.</p>
<p>Under Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) mode, however, setting PRAGMA synchronous = NORMAL provides complete transactional safety with vastly higher throughput. In NORMAL mode, the database engine does not execute an fsync on every single transaction; it only syncs during WAL checkpoints.</p>
<p>Even if the application crashes or power is abruptly severed, the write-ahead log ensures that incomplete transactions are discarded and completed transactions remain intact. Switching to NORMAL mode in WAL environments increases write throughput by up to four hundred percent with zero risk of database file corruption.</p>
