<p>SQLite maintains an internal memory cache of recently accessed database pages. By default, this cache is sized conservatively (often just two megabytes). On modern production servers with dozens of gigabytes of RAM, under-sizing the page cache forces the engine to repeatedly read index and table pages from disk.</p>
<p>Configuring PRAGMA cache_size = -64000 instructs SQLite to allocate sixty-four megabytes of memory per database connection for caching pages. Negative values represent memory in kibibytes, ensuring predictable RAM utilization across thread pools.</p>
<p>Additionally, enabling memory-mapped I/O via PRAGMA mmap_size = 2147483648 (allocating up to two gigabytes of address space) allows read queries to access database pages directly via pointer offsets, bypassing kernel system calls and eliminating user-space memory copies entirely.</p>
