<p>In an embedded database where CPU and storage are shared on the same host, unoptimized queries directly impact application responsiveness. If a query performs a full table scan across hundreds of thousands of rows, it consumes CPU cycles and evicts valuable cached pages from the OS page cache.</p>
<p>Production discipline demands regular profiling using EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN. The query planner output must confirm that every candidate query uses an index lookup (SEARCH TABLE) rather than a table scan (SCAN TABLE).</p>
<p>Furthermore, utilizing covering indexes—indexes that include all columns requested by a SELECT query—allows SQLite to satisfy the query entirely from the B-Tree index without ever loading the primary table rows from disk, cutting query execution times to sub-millisecond durations.</p>
<blockquote><p>Tune your storage pragmas to respect physical hardware, and your application will effortlessly outpace distributed complexity.</p></blockquote>
