<h2>State Hydration, Partial Reloads, and Preserving Scroll Positions: Fine-Tuning Data Fetching and Reactive View Hydration</h2>
<p>In data-dense web applications—such as analytics dashboards, feed readers, and multi-tab publication hubs—fetching the entire page state on every user interaction is unnecessarily wasteful. If a user toggles an editorial tab or applies a tag filter, re-querying and re-transmitting heavy sidebar widgets, user profile metadata, and notification counts wastes server compute and network bandwidth. In this second installment, we master Inertia partial reloads, lazy evaluation of expensive props, and fine-grained scroll preservation, demonstrating how to craft high-performance data views that feel as instantaneous as native desktop software.</p>
<h2>The Mechanics of Partial Reloads and the "only" Array</h2>
<p>When navigating or refreshing data in Inertia, the client can specify an "only" parameter containing an array of specific prop keys that should be re-evaluated. When the server detects this header, it executes only the database queries corresponding to those designated keys, returning a surgical JSON response containing solely the requested data.</p>
<p>For example, when a reader clicks between "For You", "Featured", and "Recent" tabs on the Inkwell home feed, the client requests only the "posts" prop. The server bypasses querying categories, top authors, and trending topics, executing only the feed query and updating the feed list without re-rendering surrounding layout components.</p>
<p>This selective hydration reduces server response payloads from hundreds of kilobytes down to a few kilobytes, drastically reducing CPU serialization overhead and delivering snappy sub-50ms tab switching.</p>
<h2>Lazy Evaluation with Closures: Deferring Expensive Queries</h2>
<p>In standard controller actions, executing complex database queries upfront slows down initial page load times. Inertia provides lazy evaluation by wrapping expensive prop values in anonymous PHP closures or Inertia::lazy() callbacks.</p>
<p>When a prop is defined as a closure, Laravel only executes the underlying database query if the client explicitly requests that prop via a partial reload. If the user never opens a secondary tab or modal, the expensive query is never executed.</p>
<p>This lazy evaluation pattern permits developers to co-locate secondary view data within the primary controller without paying a performance penalty on initial page entry, keeping controller code consolidated and intuitive.</p>
<h2>Preserving Scroll Position Across Stateful Mutations</h2>
<p>A common frustration in web applications is losing one's scroll position when performing inline actions—such as liking an essay, bookmarking a post, or submitting a comment. Standard browser navigation scrolls to the top of the viewport upon receiving a new page state.</p>
<p>Inertia provides the preserveScroll option across all router visit options and form submissions. When enabled, Inertia measures the exact window and container scroll coordinates prior to executing the network request and restores them immediately upon DOM patching.</p>
<p>This scroll preservation ensures that readers remain completely anchored in their reading flow. You can bookmark five articles down a long feed without the viewport jumping or flickering, delivering a polished, native reading experience.</p>
<h2>Remembering Transient Form and Scroll State Across History</h2>
<p>When users navigate away from an active form to reference an external article and subsequently return via the browser back button, losing their half-written draft creates immense frustration. Inertia provides the useRemember composable to persist component state across history visits.</p>
<p>By registering form draft state with useRemember, Inertia caches the text in browser session storage tied to the history visit state. When the user navigates backward, the form hydrates immediately with their uncommitted input intact.</p>
<p>This history preservation turns web navigation into a forgiving, resilient environment where accidental clicks never result in lost writing or reset filter configurations.</p>
<blockquote><p>Do not re-fetch the entire world when the user merely asked for the next page of results.</p></blockquote>
<h3>Practical Principles and Actionable Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
<li>Utilize Inertia partial reloads with the "only" option to update specific view sections without re-querying static layouts.</li>
<li>Wrap expensive or secondary data queries in closures (Inertia::lazy()) to defer execution until explicitly requested.</li>
<li>Always set preserveScroll: true on inline mutations such as likes, bookmarks, and pagination increments.</li>
<li>Use preserveState: true to maintain local component reactive variables across background data refreshes.</li>
<li>Leverage the useRemember composable to protect half-written drafts and filter selections across history navigation.</li>
<li>Monitor network transfer payloads to ensure partial responses transmit only the minimum candidate JSON bytes.</li>
</ul>
