<p>A subtle driver of long-term churn is product bloat. In an attempt to please every vocal user, inexperienced founders continually bolt on bespoke features, turning a once-elegant tool into a cluttered labyrinth of confusing checkboxes and menus.</p>
<p>Every added feature introduces cognitive overhead for all users and expands the maintenance surface area for the founder. Enduring software products retain their simplicity through ruthless feature curation.</p>
<p>When a founder says no to ninety percent of feature requests, they protect the core user experience that attracted their best customers in the first place, ensuring that the software remains fast, focused, and intuitive across decades.</p>
<blockquote><p>Retention is won in the quiet details: fast page loads, responsive interactions, and the relentless removal of user friction.</p></blockquote>
