<h2>The Suprachiasmatic Nucleus and Morning Photons</h2>
<p>At the base of the hypothalamus sits the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN), a cluster of approximately twenty thousand neurons that functions as the central circadian pacemaker of the human body. The primary input to the SCN is photon transmission detected by intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells (ipRGCs), which express the photopigment melanopsin.</p>
<p>When you step outside into natural morning sunlight—even on an overcast morning—tens of thousands of lux of broad-spectrum light stimulate these melanopsin receptors. This photon trigger initiates an immediate physiological cascade: it halts lingering melatonin production, triggers a healthy morning cortisol awakening response (CAR), elevates core body temperature, and sets an internal biochemical timer that dictates the onset of melatonin release twelve to fourteen hours later.</p>
<p>Conversely, viewing light through glass windows attenuates crucial wavelengths by over eighty percent, while indoor lighting typically provides less than five hundred lux. Without a robust morning light pulse, circadian gene transcription remains blunted throughout the day, producing chronic afternoon brain fog.</p>
